WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.150
David: Welcome to LitShare.

2
00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:05.525
This is Protein Degradation English
Corner.

3
00:00:05.587 --> 00:00:06.837
Episode four.

4
00:00:07.775 --> 00:00:11.150
Nature Communications, John H. McGee.

5
00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:17.075
Trimerizer Peptides: Teaching E3 Ligases
New Targets.

6
00:00:17.825 --> 00:00:18.975
Here is the question.

7
00:00:20.075 --> 00:00:23.900
Human cells have about six hundred E3
ubiquitin ligases.

8
00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:29.900
But targeted protein degradation uses only
a handful of them.

9
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:30.975
Why?

10
00:00:31.525 --> 00:00:33.225
And how do you recruit the rest?

11
00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:40.250
Alex: That is the question a team from FOG
Pharmaceuticals and Harvard set out to
answer.

12
00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.400
The paper is in Nature Communications,
2023.

13
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:46.100
First authors Olena Tokareva and Kunhua
Li.

14
00:00:49.375 --> 00:00:53.125
Corresponding authors John McGee and
Gregory Verdine.

15
00:00:53.825 --> 00:00:58.625
Their answer is a new kind of molecule: a
trimerizer Helicon.

16
00:00:59.175 --> 00:01:00.550
David: Start with the problem.

17
00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.025
Why are most E3 ligases out of reach?

18
00:01:04.025 --> 00:01:07.300
Alex: Small molecules need a pocket to
bind.

19
00:01:07.775 --> 00:01:09.625
Most E3 ligases do not have one.

20
00:01:10.725 --> 00:01:12.575
Their surfaces are large and flat.

21
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:21.275
So of about six hundred human E3s, only a
few, like VHL and cereblon, have small
molecule ligands.

22
00:01:21.875 --> 00:01:25.125
The rest are undruggable, at least for
small molecules.

23
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.275
David: And molecular glues have the same
problem?

24
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.275
Alex: Yes, and a second one.

25
00:01:31.125 --> 00:01:36.650
To design a molecular glue, you usually
need a known binder for each protein you
want to connect.

26
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.100
If you have no binder for the E3, you have
no starting point.

27
00:01:41.325 --> 00:01:43.575
That is the bottleneck this paper attacks.

28
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.025
David: So what is a Helicon?

29
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:52.775
Alex: A Helicon is a short peptide locked
into an alpha-helical shape by a chemical
staple.

30
00:01:53.525 --> 00:01:57.450
The staple is a crosslink between two
cysteine residues.

31
00:01:58.025 --> 00:02:03.825
It holds the helix rigid, improves
stability, and helps it cross cell
membranes.

32
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:11.525
And because a helix is much bigger than a
small molecule, it can grip large, flat
protein surfaces.

33
00:02:12.625 --> 00:02:13.425
David: And a trimerizer?

34
00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:24.450
Alex: A trimerizer is a Helicon that binds
two proteins at once, an E3 ligase and a
target protein, and holds them together in
a three-way complex.

35
00:02:24.975 --> 00:02:26.250
A ternary complex.

36
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.275
The E3 does not bind the target on its
own.

37
00:02:37.825 --> 00:02:39.825
The Helicon creates the interaction.

38
00:02:40.375 --> 00:02:40.975
David: How do you find one?

39
00:02:42.475 --> 00:02:44.350
You cannot just guess the sequence.

40
00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.375
Alex: You screen for it.

41
00:02:46.925 --> 00:02:49.800
The team built a two-step phage display
method.

42
00:02:51.650 --> 00:03:01.950
Step one: take a naive library of about
one hundred million Helicons, each
fourteen amino acids long, and screen it
against the E3 ligase.

43
00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:05.650
That gives you Helicons that bind the E3.

44
00:03:06.225 --> 00:03:06.725
David: And step two?

45
00:03:08.025 --> 00:03:12.000
Alex: You look at the winners and ask
which residues actually touch the E3.

46
00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:21.600
Then you build a second, focused library:
twenty amino acids long, with the
E3-binding residues fixed and everything
else randomized.

47
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:29.000
You screen that library against the target
protein, twice: once with the E3 present,
once without.

48
00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:33.975
David: And the trimerizers are the ones
that only bind when the E3 is there?

49
00:03:34.725 --> 00:03:35.100
Alex: Exactly.

50
00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:42.325
If a Helicon binds the target only in the
presence of the E3, it must be binding
cooperatively.

51
00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:45.975
The E3 and the target help each other hold
on.

52
00:03:46.825 --> 00:03:50.175
Helicons that bind the target on their own
are thrown out.

53
00:03:50.725 --> 00:03:54.025
David: No prior binder, no crystal
structure needed?

54
00:03:54.025 --> 00:03:55.175
Alex: That is the point.

55
00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.250
The whole method runs on two phage screens
and a DNA sequencer.

56
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.000
Standard molecular biology equipment.

57
00:04:03.525 --> 00:04:07.475
No rational design, no known ligands, no
structural information.

58
00:04:07.975 --> 00:04:12.750
David: Before the trimerizers, they
surveyed the E3s themselves?

59
00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:14.125
Alex: Yes.

60
00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:19.125
They screened the naive library against
E3s from all four major families.

61
00:04:19.825 --> 00:04:24.325
HECT, Cullin-RING, RING/U-box, and RBR.

62
00:04:24.325 --> 00:04:28.075
They found Helicon binders for every
family.

63
00:04:29.425 --> 00:04:34.125
WWP1 and WWP2 from the HECT family.

64
00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:38.700
CUL5 and VHL from the Cullin-RING family.

65
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:43.500
MDM2 and CHIP from the RING/U-box family.

66
00:04:43.975 --> 00:04:48.800
And RNF31 from the RBR family, from
earlier work.

67
00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:50.600
David: How strong was the binding?

68
00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:55.175
Alex: Mostly micromolar, which is typical
for a first-round screen.

69
00:04:55.175 --> 00:05:07.075
The best hit against the HECT ligase WWP2,
Helicon H three oh six, bound with a KD of
three hundred and ninety nanomolar.

70
00:05:07.775 --> 00:05:14.775
Against CUL5, Helicon H three fourteen
bound at five hundred and thirty
nanomolar.

71
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:16.425
David: Did they get structures?

72
00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:17.750
Alex: Several.

73
00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:28.175
Five co-crystal structures with the HECT
ligases, showing three distinct binding
sites on the WWP2 HECT domain.

74
00:05:28.175 --> 00:05:31.925
One Helicon binds near the E2 interface.

75
00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:39.075
Others sit at the hinge between the two
lobes, where they may block the motion the
enzyme needs to work.

76
00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:40.950
David: Now the trimerizers.

77
00:05:41.925 --> 00:05:42.825
Three examples?

78
00:05:43.725 --> 00:05:44.025
Alex: Three.

79
00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:47.400
First, CHIP and TEAD4.

80
00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:52.275
CHIP is a widely expressed RING/U-box E3.

81
00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:57.775
TEAD4 is a transcription factor, an
effector of the Hippo pathway.

82
00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:08.300
Helicon H three twenty-one drove ternary
complex formation with an EC fifty of
seven hundred and forty nanomolar.

83
00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:11.225
David: And they confirmed the
cooperativity?

84
00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.200
Alex: By SPR, surface plasmon resonance.

85
00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:20.575
CHIP bound TEAD4 only when the Helicon was
present.

86
00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:25.475
The ternary KD was one hundred and fifty
nanomolar.

87
00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:35.650
And H three twenty-one gave essentially
complete ternary complex formation, one
hundred and one percent of the expected
signal.

88
00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:37.900
David: Did it do anything functional?

89
00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:38.975
Alex: Yes.

90
00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:43.325
TEAD4 normally binds YAP1, a coactivator.

91
00:06:46.075 --> 00:06:50.550
H three twenty-one disrupted that
interaction, but only when CHIP was
present.

92
00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:55.000
So the trimerizer does not just glue two
proteins together.

93
00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:57.575
It rewires a real interaction.

94
00:06:58.175 --> 00:06:59.400
David: Second example?

95
00:07:01.075 --> 00:07:06.175
Alex: CHIP and PPIA, cyclophilin A, an
enzyme that helps proteins fold.

96
00:07:07.025 --> 00:07:13.125
Helicon H three twenty-six gave an EC
fifty of five hundred and fifty nanomolar.

97
00:07:13.775 --> 00:07:22.775
And the ternary complex was blocked by
cyclosporine A, which binds PPIA, and
weakened by a CHIP-binding Helicon.

98
00:07:23.575 --> 00:07:26.200
That pins down where the trimerizer sits.

99
00:07:26.675 --> 00:07:27.575
David: And the third?

100
00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.725
Alex: MDM2 and beta-catenin.

101
00:07:31.425 --> 00:07:37.875
Beta-catenin is a central player in Wnt
signaling, often dysregulated in cancer.

102
00:07:37.875 --> 00:07:54.700
Three trimerizers, H three thirty, H three
thirty-two, and H three thirty-four, drove
ternary complex formation with EC fifty
values of one hundred, sixty-six, and
sixty-seven nanomolar.

103
00:07:55.075 --> 00:07:56.650
David: How selective were they?

104
00:07:57.350 --> 00:07:57.750
Alex: Very.

105
00:07:58.425 --> 00:08:05.825
MDM4 is a close relative of MDM2, about
fifty-five percent identical in the
binding domain.

106
00:08:06.725 --> 00:08:09.825
None of the trimerizers worked with MDM4.

107
00:08:10.725 --> 00:08:12.150
Only MDM2.

108
00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:14.700
David: They solved structures of those
too?

109
00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:20.975
Alex: Yes, two ternary complexes by X-ray
crystallography.

110
00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:25.925
H three thirty at two point six angstroms,
H three thirty-two at three point nine.

111
00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:39.750
Both show the Helicon bridging the
p53-binding site of MDM2 and the ICAT-
binding site of beta-catenin.

112
00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:42.475
David: What does the structure actually
show?

113
00:08:42.475 --> 00:08:51.025
Alex: The Helicon's side chains plug into
a hydrophobic cleft on MDM2, the same
cleft that p53 uses.

114
00:08:51.775 --> 00:08:55.325
On the beta-catenin side, it forms
hydrogen bonds.

115
00:08:56.175 --> 00:09:02.250
One residue, aspartate nineteen of H three
thirty, touches both proteins at once.

116
00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:04.525
It is a literal bridge.

117
00:09:04.875 --> 00:09:07.925
David: And the two proteins touch each
other directly?

118
00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:09.150
Alex: They do.

119
00:09:09.825 --> 00:09:16.000
Direct contacts between MDM2 and beta-
catenin, hydrogen bonds and salt bridges.

120
00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.925
That is the signature of cooperativity.

121
00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:21.850
The three components stabilize each other.

122
00:09:22.225 --> 00:09:25.625
David: One thing that caught my eye: no
hook effect.

123
00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:26.700
Alex: Right.

124
00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:32.700
With a classic heterobifunctional
molecule, too much drug saturates both
proteins separately, and ternary complex
formation drops.

125
00:11:06.625 --> 00:11:07.775
A bell-shaped curve.

126
00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:42.550
The control chimeric peptides in this
paper showed exactly that.

127
00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.550
But the trimerizers kept climbing.

128
00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.875
Cooperative binding avoids the hook.

129
00:12:49.175 --> 00:12:50.475
David: Now the limitation.

130
00:12:51.425 --> 00:12:52.700
This is all in vitro?

131
00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:54.100
Alex: All of it.

132
00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:59.000
Binding assays, TR-FRET, SPR,
crystallography.

133
00:12:59.675 --> 00:13:00.675
No cell data.

134
00:13:01.225 --> 00:13:03.825
No ubiquitylation, no degradation.

135
00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:13.125
The authors are clear: the next step is to
get these Helicons into cells and test
whether they can actually drive
degradation.

136
00:13:14.175 --> 00:13:16.950
David: So this is not yet a degrader
story.

137
00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:18.225
Alex: Not yet.

138
00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:21.100
It is a ternary complex story.

139
00:13:21.675 --> 00:13:28.850
The trimerizers prove you can reprogram an
E3 surface to recognize a new target,
cooperatively, from scratch.

140
00:13:29.825 --> 00:13:32.200
Degradation is the next chapter, not this
one.

141
00:13:33.375 --> 00:13:33.925
David: What is the takeaway?

142
00:13:35.025 --> 00:13:35.450
Alex: Two things.

143
00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:37.625
First, the method is general.

144
00:13:38.275 --> 00:13:43.850
Any E3 you can screen, any target you can
purify, you can hunt for a trimerizer.

145
00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:48.275
Second, it expands the E3 toolbox.

146
00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:52.350
Six hundred E3s, most of them untouched.

147
00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:59.850
If even a fraction can be reprogrammed
this way, targeted protein degradation
gets much bigger.

148
00:14:00.325 --> 00:14:04.250
David: A method paper, then, with three
proofs of concept.

149
00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:06.025
Alex: Exactly.

150
00:14:06.925 --> 00:14:10.525
And a reminder that peptides can go where
small molecules cannot.

151
00:14:11.275 --> 00:14:14.350
Large, flat surfaces are not undruggable.

152
00:14:14.825 --> 00:14:16.250
They just need a bigger binder.

153
00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:18.700
David: That is it for this episode.

154
00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:23.775
You have been listening to Protein
Degradation English Corner, from Litshare.

155
00:14:24.525 --> 00:14:25.425
See you next time.
